Institutions provide constraints for multiple actors of contending interests to reach a collective choice. Examples include the War Powers Act, the Constitution, and elections.



# Foreign Policy
National interests during Obama's term was inspired by the U.S.' national identity:
- Protecting human rights
- Promoting democracy
- Economic equity
- Women's rights
- Environmentalism

In his farewell address, Washington wished that the U.S. distance itself from foreign entanglements. This is because the Federalists and Republicans were aligning with either Great Britain and France, potentially destabilizing the new American democracy.

Right after 9/11, Congress authorized the president to use military force against associated organizations. This AUMF would later be cited by President Obama in 2014 in order to use military force against ISIS, even though he declared the war on Al Qaeda three years prior.

Obama had an EO that would have provided temporary legal status to undocumented immigrants provided they had no criminal history. This was blocked in court, arguing that he was overstepping his authority. Trump tried to create EO that reversed Obama's EO and blocked immigrants from countries deemed to have high levels of terrorist activity, but this was blocked because it was discriminatory against Muslims. 

Republicans used to be more aligned with primacy compared to Democrats, but ever since Trump, isolationism has risen inside the party.



# Coercion
After fighting Iraq, the U.S. is beginning to seem like it's using its international coercive power for predation. Trump has seemed to reel this back by pulling the U.S. out of Syria and Afghanistan, but his threats of exiting NATO reignited fears.

<!-- a dispute between GB and France altered the relationship between GB and the colonies, which lead to the colonies teaming up with France to fight GB -->
Seven Years War/French-Indian War: British victory over France and Spain in North America. UK Gained significant territory in North America like French Canada. Additionally, more territory to defend meant doubling the number of troops deployed. UK was under a lot of debt from war and this, so they imposed many taxes on the colonies (e.g., Stamp Act). Resistance followed. The colonies would later team up with French to fight Great Britain. 

Iraq is in financial turmoil due to a war with Iran. It wants Kuwait to cut its oil production to raise oil prices. Iraq made a sunk cost signal by lining up troops on Kuwait's borders, but it was ineffective because Saddam Hussein told Egypt that Iraq was exaggerating efforts. Therefore, Kuwait didn't think Iraq had high resolve when it in fact did (private information). This led to war. When the U.S. joined on Kuwait's side, Iraq had incorrectly believed that the U.S. had low resolve.

World War I brought major devastation in Europe. This left the U.S. the strongest on all measures. It was caused by a commitment problem between a declining Austria-Hungary versus Russia. When alliance obligations with Austria-Hungary pull Germany into the war, it also ended up launching a preventative war against Russia. This is because Germany worried that the distribution of power was shifting away from it. Similarly, Germany provoked the U.S. into reversing course on isolationism because it feared the untouched U.S. was going inevitably join the war at some point, so interfering with U.S. trade with Great Britain and France may have been the necessary cut to supply lines that spark a victory. Additionally, President Wilson thought fighting in the war would give the U.S. the ability to end multinational empires (e.g., Austria-Hungary). The war did just that and also the creation of a collective security system (i.e., League of Nations, member states try to maintain the status quo in Europe). The U.S. was also on top in terms of economy, serving as a lender to Europe and making up the production of many goods of Europe.

On the negotiating table for the Treaty of Versailles, President Wilson championed for self-determination/anti-imperialism, democracy, and collective security (i.e., League of Nations). That last idea would generate international governance that would help states cooperate to defend the maintenance of the status quo. Truman and FDR carried this ideas in the post-WWII settlement: 
- Foster democracy in Germany and Japan
- The Marshall Plan would give aid to a vulnerable Europe to twart the growth of communism
- NATO to preempt something like Nazi Germany again
- International Monetary Fund, World Bank, World Trade Organization: create an environment for free trade

After World War II, the U.S. and Soviet Union became the great powers in a bipolar system. They maintained their coalitions by the threat of invasion by the other coalition. Not to mention that both sides had nukes. Bipolarity did also create a competition between the systems of democratic capitalism versus authoritarian communism. This led to the collapse of the USSR because its system demanded perpetual expansion (which U.S. containment policy kept in check).